<h2>The dispute</h2>
<p>The landlords sought eviction of a shop in Chandni Chowk under Section 14(1)(e) read with Section 25-B of the Delhi Rent Control Act, 1958, pleading bona fide requirement. The tenants filed the mandatory application for leave to defend. While arguments on that application were pending, they moved a further application under Section 151 CPC to bring on record what they called subsequent developments: the fate of other eviction petitions the landlords had filed against other shops in the same premises — several withdrawn or disposed of — and photographs of banners advertising shops for rent. The Rent Controller dismissed the application on 6 October 2026, and the tenants invoked Article 227.</p>

<h2>The rival positions</h2>
<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><strong>Tenants</strong><p>The landlords breached a 2010 High Court direction to disclose related litigation; the withdrawn petitions and the “available for rent” banners undermine the claimed need; the material is recent and goes to the root of bona fides.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><strong>Landlords</strong><p>The other petitions and the need for the entire premises were disclosed; the photographs were staged; five of the eight shops shown remain tenanted and under litigation; the application is a device to protract the summary proceeding.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>What the Court held</h2>
<p>Justice Ajay Digpaul dismissed the petition on stacked grounds. The explanation for the delayed filing was vague — the e-courts records the tenants relied on were publicly accessible well before. The developments themselves were not established: nothing showed the landlords had actually re-let the other shops. The 2010 disclosure direction covered only the same property, which the other shops were not. And on the governing test from <em>Gaya Prasad</em>, subsequent events displace a landlord’s bona fide requirement only when the need is completely eclipsed — a threshold this material did not approach, with <em>Gulab Singh</em> and <em>Punjab Stainless</em> distinguished on their facts.</p>
<p>The judgment’s sharpest line is procedural: the summary procedure envisaged under Section 25-B of the DRC Act cannot be bypassed under Section 151 CPC. Leave to defend is a single, front-loaded opportunity; inherent powers do not convert it into an open-ended filing window.</p>

<h2>Article 227: the supervisory lens</h2>
<div class="note">
<p>The Court reiterated that supervisory jurisdiction is confined to cases of blatant violation of fundamental tenets of law — perversity, patent illegality, jurisdictional error. A plausible and well-reasoned view of the Rent Controller, even if another view were possible, is not a ground for interference. Petitions under Article 227 against interlocutory rent-control orders therefore need to identify a defect in the decision-making process, not merely re-argue the application.</p>
</div>

<h2>Takeaways for tenants and landlords</h2>
<p>For tenants: everything that can support leave to defend must go into the leave-to-defend application itself — litigation searches, photographs, disclosures — because the summary scheme offers no routine second chance, and genuinely new events must meet the demanding “complete eclipse” standard. For landlords: candid disclosure of related litigation at the outset forecloses the most common line of collateral attack. And for both: the ultimate safeguard against a feigned requirement is not an interlocutory skirmish but Section 19, which penalises a landlord who evicts and then fails to occupy.</p>
<p>The High Court clarified that its observations do not touch the merits of the eviction petition, which remains pending before the Rent Controller.</p>
